\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MARKETS 2-Leu hits two-month low, Polish PMI data supports zloty\b0\par\par\uc2 1,018 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Juni 2013\par 15:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Romania sells bonds as planned, yields edge up\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Retreat from Romanian debt hits leu\par     * PMIs help zloty, forint, but rate outlooks hurt\par     By Jason Hovet\par     PRAGUE, June 3 (Reuters) - The leu hit a two-month low on\par Monday, dropping for a second day as investors started to\par retreat from Romanian bonds and as concerns over how long global\par stimulus programmes would last rattled central European markets.\par     The Polish zloty - hard hit in the past week - steadied off\par recent lows after a purchasing managers' survey showed a slight\par pickup in manufacturing activity in Poland although the prospect\par of lower interest rates still weighed on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Central European markets have become more volatile in the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 past month with investors rattled by uncertainty over when the U.S. Federal Reserve might slow its stimulus programme - part of the "wall of money" that has fuelled investor appetite for emerging market assets. Any cutback would hit the region. Romania sold a planned 500 million lei ($147.5 million) in five-year treasury bonds on Monday, with the average accepted yield up 13 basis points from two weeks ago. Analysts said investors were starting to back off Romanian debt, reversing a rally seen earlier in the year that had sent yields in Romania and around central Europe to record lows. Czech 10-year yields hit a two-month high on Monday. Moves in Romania's smaller debt markets can have a bigger impact on the leu than elsewhere in the region and the retreat has caused the leu to lose 1.1 percent since Thursday's close. It bid down 0.3 percent on the day at 4.402 to the euro and had weakened as far as 4.42 before the auction. "The weakening appears to be a short squeeze ... and now some profit taking after yields at the bond tender were somewhat higher," one trader in Bucharest said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     \par     RATE CUTS WATCHED\par     The zloty was up less than 0.1 percent to bid at\par 4.266 to the euro - after touching a session low of 4.28. The\par forint also inched up 0.1 percent to 295.4. Both were\par just off 11-month and one-month lows, respectively.\par     A Polish manufacturing purchasing managers' index (PMI)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 edged up slightly in May but business activity still shrank for the 14th month running. Hungarian manufacturing also contracted, according to a PMI survey, but Czech manufacturing grew last month for the first time in more than a year. The zloty has weakened on expectations the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will cut borrowing costs by a quarter percentage point to a new low of 2.75 percent at a meeting on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     "EUR/HUF and EUR/PLN continue to be weak given the prospect\par of further monetary policy easing ... and a global environment\par characterised by increasing uncertainty with regards to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy," Raiffeisen analysts said in a note.\par     "In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , we would also not exclude verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par favour of the zloty in the days ahead."\par     Some analysts said the recent weakness in the zloty might\par prompt the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to hold fire on rates for now. A Reuters\par poll last week, though, showed 17 of 19 analysts expected a cut\par on Wednesday. \par     In May, the bank defied analysts' forecasts and cut rates in\par response to data that showed the economy was hovering on the\par brink of recession. \par     Elsewhere, the Czech crown was a touch weaker\par after benefitting last week from the unwinding of short\par positions versus the zloty and forint, helping it recover from a\par three-year low. \par     Analysts see further downside for the crown given a still\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 weak Czech economy, stuck in its longest recession in two decades, and persistent threats from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that it could \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if it needs to ease monetary policy now that interest rates are near zero. Stock markets in central Europe fell by up to 1 percent because of concerns over the U.S. Fed's bond-buying programme. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   CEE MARKETS SNAPSHOT AT 1358 CET\par  ************************** \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                              bid     close      change  in 2013\par  Czech crown                 25.739    25.697   -0.16%  -2.68%\par  Hungarian forint           295.360   295.600  +0.08%  -1.49%\par  Polish zloty                 4.267     4.270   +0.07%  -4.52%\par  Romanian leu                 4.402     4.388   -0.30%  +0.94%\par  Croatian kuna                7.539     7.550   +0.14% +0.16%\par  Serbian dinar              113.040   112.710    -0.29%   -0.65%\par  Note: daily change calculated from previous close at 1600 GMT\par  **************************** STOCKS **************************\par                              Latest  Previous   Daily   Change\par                                      close      change  in 2013\par  Prague                      957.46    957.86   -0.04%  -