\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Poland cuts rates again, flags more easing in January\b0\par\par\uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2012\par 13:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cuts rates by 25 bps, as expected\par \par \uc2 * Governor signals another cut in January\par \par \uc2 * Bank to stick to policy of gradual policy easing\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 By Karolina Slowikowska\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Dec 5 (Reuters) - \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut interest rates for the second consecutive month on Wednesday and signalled more cuts in January and beyond to help an economy that has slowed to its lowest pace in three years.\par \par \uc2 The bank's 10-strong Monetary Policy Council (MPC) cut rates by 25 basis points as widely expected, bringing the key rate in the largest central European economy to 4.25 percent, its lowest since May 2011.\par \par \uc2 Poland, the sole \uc2 European Union\uc2  member to avoid a recession since 2008, joined other regional central banks only last month in starting to easing monetary policy, and some analysts say it has acted too late to tackle the deepening slump.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Marek Belka said on Wednesday the situation was not "dramatic" and recession highly unlikely in an economy that has seen uninterrupted expansion for two decades.\par \par \uc2 "Changes in rates should be gradual unless some dramatic events take place," Belka told a news conference after the decision, adding the MPC did not want to surprise the markets with a larger cut.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the possibility of another cut in January, Belka said: "The Council is working hard and can't afford a pause in easing."\par \par \uc2 The "amazing" stability of the zloty \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which benefits the economy, may reflect Poland's shrinking current account deficit, Belka said, also hinting it could be a result of past \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SLOWDOWN\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic growth slowed to a much lower-than-expected 1.4 percent year-on-year in the third quarter, prompting the government to acknowledge expansion this year would come in below its 2.5 percent forecast.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects the economy to slow to 1.5 percent next year, which would be its lowest growth rate since 2002, while the MPC said in its statement that growth in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's largest eastern economy was likely to be "moderate" over the next few years.\par \par \uc2 But when pressed on the possible scale of the monetary easing cycle ahead, Belka reiterated that the majority of the Council preferred to keep interest rates above inflation, unless there was an acute crisis in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Price growth eased to an annual 3.4 percent in October and for the first time in nearly two years fell below the upper limit of the bank's 1.5-3.5 percent permitted fluctuation band.\par \par \uc2 "A larger cut was probably also justified but nobody really expected the Monetary Policy Council to have enough courage to make a bolder move," said Piotr Bielski, a senior economist at \uc2 Bank Zachodni WBK\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The zloty inched up after the decision, while bond yields fell, reflecting expectations of further policy easing.\par \par \uc2 "As the economy is weakening and monetary easing is very likely to continue, we expect the EUR/PLN to slightly drop to 4.15 at the end of the year," Erstegroup said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The zloty traded at 4.1217 to the euro by 1709 GMT.\par \par \uc2 The market expects a further 75 basis points to be chopped off the main rate in the next nine months, although pressure has been mounting for more decisive action to help ease credit for households and companies.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the region, recession-stricken Hungary has cut rates four times by a total of 100 basis points, while the Czech Republic has hinted at \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the country's interest rates are already near zero.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting By Karolina Slowikowska; Writing by Marcin Goettig and Karolina Slowikowska; Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-RATES/ (UPDATE 2) | ABN | D | E | RBN | G | M | PX | RNP | DNP | PGE   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121205e8c5000lr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
