\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Czech, Polish manufacturing falls, outlook weak\b0\par\par\uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2012\par 12:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Polish, Czech PMIs rise to 48.2, still show business shrinking\par \par \uc2 * Analysts say data point to further contraction in forth quarter\par \par \uc2 * Data suggests more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  easing, Poland to cut on Wednesday\par \par \uc2 By Michael Winfrey\par \par \uc2 PRAGUE, Dec 3 (Reuters) - Manufacturing in the EU's two biggest eastern economies shrank for the eighth straight month in November, pointing to a further slowdown in Poland and a fifth month of recession for the export-dependent Czechs.\par \par \uc2 With the euro zone debt crisis hitting consumers in their main export markets, the countries of central and Eastern Europe have cut output of the cars, electronics and other manufactured goods that are their main source of national income.\par \par \uc2 Combined with government austerity measures at home that have deterred consumers and companies from spending, that has extended a Czech recession that started at the end of 2011. In Poland, a once robust expansion is slowing to its weakest rate since the height of the economic crisis in 2009.\par \par \uc2 A slower decline in euro zone manufacturing, especially in the bloc's biggest economy Germany, has raised some hopes that manufacturing orders can arrest their slide some time next year, but analysts said Monday's data indicated recovery was still far away.\par \par \uc2 The Purchasing Managers' Index (PMI) in both Poland and the Czech Republic rose to 48.2 points in November, showing a slower decline than in the previous month but still below the 50 level that divides contraction from growth.\par \par \uc2 The figures were less weak than Poland's 47.3 in October and 47.2 for the Czechs, and well above the 47.5 predicted by analysts for both countries in Reuters polls. They showed a similar picture to the euro zone, where the manufacturing PMI in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc firmed to 46.2, from 45.4 in October. Even so, that marked a 16th month of shrinking euro zone activity.\par \par \uc2 In Hungary, where the PMI is calculated under different methodology and is often very volatile, the numbers rose to 52.3 in November, showing significant growth, from 49.9 in October. But analysts said the picture remained grim.\par \par \uc2 "The situation will not improve dramatically but rather only gradually," said Piotr Bujak, chief economist at Nordea Poland.\par \par \uc2 "This turnaround in the euro area will not be strong enough to avoid further slowdown in the Polish economy in the final months of this year and will not be strong enough to help the Czech and Hungarian economies to get out of a recession."\par \par \uc2 Economists largely consider PMI one of the best forward looking indicators for economic performance, although in Poland analysts often look more to local consumer and business surveys.\par \par \uc2 The region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were virtually flat after the data.\par \par \uc2 DECLINE\par \par \uc2 In Poland, the region's largest economy, output, new export orders and employment all declined at a slower pace.\par \par \uc2 Buoyed by its 38-million-strong consumer market, Poland is the only \uc2 European Union\uc2  state to have avoided recession since 2008. While growth has slowed dramatically, it has suffered less than its regional peers from the fall in euro zone demand.\par \par \uc2 But plummeting euro zone demand for consumer goods such as cars has spelled trouble for the Czechs, whose exports account for 85 percent of economic output.\par \par \uc2 Data showed on Monday new car registrations in France fell 19.2 percent in November, meshing with last month's announcement by a Czech car factory run by Toyota and \uc2 Peugeot Citroen\uc2  that it would cut production this year by 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 Czech new export orders fell for the 13th straight month, and on a quarterly basis, data for the fourth quarter signalled the sharpest contraction since third quarter 2009.\par \par \uc2 The Czech economy shrank 1.5 percent year-on-year from July to September, extending a recession dating from the end of 2011, and analysts said they expected no recovery before the year end.\par \par \uc2 "The Czech economy has contracted in the last four quarters to third quarter 2012, and the PMI does not point to a turn to positive growth in the last quarter of this year either," said \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Agata Urbanska\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slashed interest rates to 0.05 percent, leaving it armed with only unconventional tools such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it needs to take further action.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been more hesitant to cut quickly. But following data on Friday showing growth at 1.4 percent in the third quarter - its slowest since 2009 - analysts said they expected at least another quarter point cut to 4.25 percent on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 "The MPC could seriously consider an interest rate cut of 50 basis points, but we believe that it will feel satisfied with a cut of 25 bps, although it may be open to more monetary easing down the road," ING wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Jana Mlcochova and Karolina Slowikowska; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 CENTRALEUROPE-PMI/ (WRAPUP 1) | CZS | ABN | D | E | RBN | HX | M | PX | ABX | BNX | FUN | RNP | DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020121203e8c3000nq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
