\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Hungary Phone Tax, Poland Data In Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leos Rousek   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2012\par 03:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par Local/GMT \par  \par  \par    N/A    Hungarian parliament vote on telecom tax \par 1400/1200 Polish April corporate wages, employment \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's controversial tax on voice telephone calls and Polish economic indicators for April top the agenda Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In Hungary, lawmakers are set to vote on a new tax on telecom companies that will be based on callers' voice traffic. The tax will come on top of value-added taxes for telephone bills.\par \par \uc2 The voice tax is part of the government's deficit-cutting efforts. Telecom companies have assailed the new tax as a direct successor to the sector-specific crisis taxes the government promised to phase out by 2013.\par \par \uc2 Analysts see Polish corporate wages up more than 4% on the year in April, only slightly above the rate of consumer price inflation. The reading should make an interest rate increase less likely in the near future.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0150 GMT 25.478-524 \par Previous 2150 GMT 25.462-522 \par %Chg +0.03 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0150 GMT 297.78-8.32 \par Previous 2150 GMT 297.64-8.37 \par % Chg   +0.02 \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0150 GMT 4.3611-70 \par Previous 2150 GMT 4.3571-656 \par % Chg  +0.06 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par         Thu    Wed \par 3 yrs   8.25%  8.18% \par 5 yrs   8.35%  8.32% \par 10yrs   8.45%  8.40% \par (1) markets closed Monday \par  \par Poland \par         Thu    Wed \par 3 yrs   4.84%  4.79%(1) \par 5 yrs   5.04%  5.00% \par 10yrs   5.49%  5.47% \par (1) 2 yrs \par  \par Czech Republic \par         Thu    Wed \par 3 yrs   1.49%  1.49% \par 5 yrs   2.24%  2.24% \par 10yrs   3.26%  3.27% \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,057.12-62.61-2.95% \par  \par BUX \par 16,280.38-257.76-1.56% \par  \par PX \par 867.70-7.90-0.90% \par  \par EU Enlarged \par 1,640.94-39.29-2.34% \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: The National Bank of Poland is able to step into the zloty market at any time, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official said Thursday, but market participants doubt the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is weak enough for the bank to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 POLAND\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : More European banks, faced with pressure from the euro-zone crisis, may put their Polish units up for sale, following several such recent moves, Marek Belka, governor of the National Bank of Poland, is quoted as saying by Reuters Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: Israel and the Czech Republic inked a raft of intergovernmental deals on Thursday as Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu began a two-day visit to Prague. Israel has "no better friends in Europe than the Czech Republic," Netanyahu said following talks with his Czech counterpart Petr Necas in Prague.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: German carmaker Daimler will turn out 40,000 Mercedes B-Class models at its new plant in the Hungarian city of Kecskemet this year, Mercedes-Benz Manufacturing Hungary managing director Frank Klein told MTI on Thursday. The plant will start producing CLA coupes at the beginning of 2013.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BULGARIA: Qatar eyed Bulgaria investment opportunities on Thursday as the two countries signed a framework agreement at the start of a visit here by Qatari Prime Minister Sheikh Hamad bin Jassem Al-Thani.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Leos Rousek,