\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Greek Election Call Weighs On CEE  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney, Marcin Sobczyk and Veronika Gulyas   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 726 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2012\par 04:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TODAY'S CALENDAR \par Local/GMT \par 0800/0600 Coal miner New World Resources 1Q results \par 1000/0800 Czech current account balance for March \par 1400/1200 Poland's March current account \par 1400/1200 Hungary \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  event on economy \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central European markets will likely continue to see volatility Wednesday as investors react to the news that Greece is headed for a new round of early elections, highlighting the instability facing the euro zone.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The situation is weighing on regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though bond yields are holding steady, and together with weak economic growth in the region raises the specter of monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 The Czech and Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid to a four-month lows against the euro Tuesday and the zloty is now approaching levels that previously prompted the Polish finance ministry and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sell euros on the market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's deputy finance minister, Dominik Radziwill, early Tuesday said the ministry would return to the market to prop up the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  but as of Tuesday afternoon neither the ministry nor the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The zloty stabilized around EUR4.35 in the afternoon, a level almost 4% weaker than before the first Greek election of May 6.\par \par \uc2 The Hungarian forint tested levels around HUF293.5 versus the euro Tuesday with the Greek situation in focus but also due to a worse-than-expected first-quarter GDP data.\par \par \uc2 "The HUF295 level might prove to be a serious resistance level but a heavy wave of negative news may help break it through in the coming period," said Peter Karsai of \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mojmir Hampl, deputy governor of the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told Dow Jones Newswires Tuesday that the worse-than-expected Czech gross domestic product data increases the chance of a rate cut.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to consider anything other than further easing," Hampl said.\par \par \uc2 Economist Jaromir Sindel of Citibank said while the poor Czech GDP reading supports the doves on the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board, he doesn't expect a strong immediate policy response unless GDP details confirm the preliminary estimate without upward revisions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par EUR/CZK \par Latest 0150 GMT 25.614-74 \par Previous 2150 GMT 25.637-96 \par %Chg -0.09 \par  \par EUR/HUF \par Latest 0150 GMT 293.82-4.62 \par Previous 2150 GMT 294.05-74 \par % Chg  -0.06 \par  \par EUR/PLN \par Latest 0150 GMT 4.3644-96 \par Previous 2150 GMT 4.3638-710 \par % Chg  -0.01 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par Hungary \par       Tues  Mon \par 3 yrs 7.98% 8.07% \par 5 yrs 8.16% 8.20% \par 10yrs 8.23% 8.30% \par  \par Poland \par       Tues  Mon \par 3 yrs 4.82% 4.82%(1) \par 5 yrs 5.03% 5.01% \par 10yrs 5.47% 5.45% \par (1) 2 yrs \par  \par Czech Republic \par       Tues  Mon \par 3 yrs 1.49% 1.49% \par 5 yrs 2.24% 2.23% \par 10yrs 3.27% 3.27% \par  \par STOCKS \par  \par WIG 20 \par 2,111.15 -25.97 -1.22% \par  \par BUX \par 16,877.46 -131.86 -0.78% \par  \par PX \par 876.0  -17.2  -1.9% \par  \par EU Enlarged \par 1,681.55 -27.63 -1.62% \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CZECH REPUBLIC: A weak Czech gross domestic product reading for the first quarter, which shows Czech recession has deepened, make a rate cut the most likely future policy move by the Czech National Bank, Mojmir Hampl, its vice-governor said Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVAKIA: Slovakia's new leftist government won a confidence vote Tuesday over its plans to raise taxes and impose fiscal discipline.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's proposed amendments to its controversial media law are a step in the right direction, but more needs to be done, the chief of the Council of Europe said on Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: The EU now projects Hungary's budget deficit to be below 3% of gross domestic product this year and the next, which warrants lifting the suspension of EU funds to Hungary in 2013, the Hungarian economy minister's letter to the members of the Ecofin showed Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Sean Carney, Marcin Sobczyk and Veronika Gulyas, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 222 315 290; sean.carney@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe}}}\uc2 \f2  for the new Dow Jones blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region. [ 05-15-12 2212ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020120516e85g0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
