\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Poland may issue Swiss-franc debt in April, eurobonds by summer\b0\par\par\uc2 627 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 April 2012\par 07:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Poland has met 56 pct of 2012 borrowing needs - deputy finmin Radziwill\par \par \uc2 * Most EU funds will be swapped at \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months\par \par \uc2 * Poland's economy may expand 4 pct in Q1, around 3 pct in 2012\par \par \uc2 By Dagmara Leszkowicz and Pawel Sobczak\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, April 10 (Reuters) - Poland may issue a small amount of Swiss franc-denominated bonds as early as this month, and it is mulling a benchmark bond issue either in dollars or euros before the summer, deputy finance minister Dominik Radziwill told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2 Poland, the largest economy in Europe's eastern wing, has already met 56 percent its 2012 borrowing needs and plans to reach two-thirds by the end of the first half, Radziwill said in comments authorised for release on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "If market conditions are favourable, we will hold a Swiss franc issue worth several hundred million (francs)," Radziwill said, adding that Warsaw may also get a headstart on its 2013 borrowing requirement.\par \par \uc2 "I assume that we will do some 2013 prefinancing, but the level will be lower compared to the previous year."\par \par \uc2 Radziwill kept up the threat of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the zloty, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  could exchange EU funds for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market if necessary.\par \par \uc2 A flood of cheap money from major central banks, most recently the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has fuelled demand for Polish bonds and other emerging market debt since the start of the year, and Polish yields hit multi-year lows in mid-March.\par \par \uc2 But hawkish rhetoric from the national bank and the prospect of cheap money supply from the Fed and other major central banks drying up as their economies improve have led Polish yields to rise some 20 basis points since.\par \par \uc2 That rhetoric has not helped the Polish zloty, however, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - along with regional peers - has been under pressure as concern over the euro zone's debt problems kept investors away from riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 In 2011, the finance ministry together with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the joint action of exchanging EU funds through state-owned bank BGK for zloty.\par \par \uc2 This year, both institutions have announced plans to reduce their activity on the spot market in the second and third quarter, although Radziwill kept open the option of direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "When it comes to exchanging EU funds, we make current decisions whether they should be exchanged at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  or in the market," Radziwill said.\par \par \uc2 "I assume most funds will be exchanged at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming months. But we always have a possibility of exchanging funds on the spot market if we see such a need."\par \par \uc2 UPGRADE?\par \par \uc2 The minister was optimistic about the growth prospects and ratings outlook for the biggest of the European Union's ex-communist economies.\par \par \uc2 "(Poland's) economy will probably expand by more than 3 percent in the whole of 2012, while in the first quarter it should be around 4 percent," Radziwill said.\par \par \uc2 The full-year forecast is in line with analysts estimates, but they expect less growth in the first three months of 2012 - around 3.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 These prospects and Warsaw's fiscal steps aimed at reducing the general government deficit to below 3 percent this year should help improve Poland's outlook at credit rating agencies, the minister said.\par \par \uc2 "I don't rule out that ratings' improvement may take place as early as in the fourth quarter (of 2012)," Radziwill said.\par \par \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  and Fitch currently rate Poland at A- with stable outlooks, while \uc2 Moody's\uc2  A2, also with stable outlook.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Hugh Lawson)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-RADZIWILL/ (INTERVIEW) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | PX   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120413e84a0007b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
