\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Polish c.bank may buy bonds only if deep crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Januar 2012\par 15:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Recasts with c.bank comments, analyst)\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Jan 2 (Reuters) - Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only buy the country's debt on the secondary market if the domestic financial sector were at risk, it said on Monday, clarifying its stance after the finance minister suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could buy bonds if Polish yields rose sharply.\par \par \uc2 Earlier on Monday Finance Minister Jacek Rostowski, in an interview with the Polish edition of Newsweek magazine, said it might be adviseable for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBP) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the secondary debt market if \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's debt yields rose sharply on the back of market panic or a crisis.\par \par \uc2 "The main goal of the NBP is to keep prices stable," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in a statement. "The independent \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  supports the government's economic policy, but cannot fill in for it, especially as regards public debt management."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Poland's constitution bans direct financing of the country's budget deficit by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but said the bank was also obliged to support the stability of the financial sector.\par \par \uc2 "That means NBP purchases of debt on the secondary market could only take place under extraordinary circumstances if the financial sector's stability was endangered," it read.\par \par \uc2 "The current macroeconomic conditions and Poland's financial situation (...) show the financial sector's stability is not threatened by the state of public finances."\par \par \uc2 So far, the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not bought bonds on the secondary market.\par \par \uc2 However, Warsaw used state-owned bank BGK to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the debt market last September, buying bonds in a move that soothed market nervousness and sent yields on longer-dated papers several basis points lower.\par \par \uc2 "NBP debt purchases is in fact printing money and that would spark a huge depreciation pressure on the zloty and cause serious troubles for the monetary policy. So one should be wary of that," said a Warsaw-based dealer.\par \par \uc2 "At this point there is no market need for such an \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there is no reason for any large-scale unloading of the Polish debt," the dealer added. "Yields on Polish debt are still relatively low compared to what is going on elsewhere."\par \par \uc2 The yield on Polish 10-year paper is hovering around 5.88 percent, well below that of countries' on the euro zone periphery, which have seen borrowing costs soar in the past year as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc's sovereign debt crisis deepened.\par \par \uc2 But analysts say foreign investors, who now hold some 31 percent of Poland's total outstanding domestic bonds, could shift away from central and east European assets, which as emerging market assets are riskier than core European ones, should the economic crisis in Europe deepen significantly.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Dagmara Leszkowicz and Gabriela Baczynska; Editing by Susan Fenton)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-BONDS/ (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020120105e812000rk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
