\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: European Sovereign CDS Steady\b0\par\par\uc2 1,358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Dezember 2011\par 10:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2011, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0905 GMT [Dow Jones] European sovereign CDS are steady, alongside a similar quiet tone in the cash bond market, as volumes remain light with traders coasting into the end of the year. Italy's five-year CDS is unchanged at 483 bps and Spain is 1 bp tighter at 379 bps, according to Markit. France is 1 bps tighter at 216 bps, Belgium is 5 bps tighter at 309 bps, and Germany is 1 bp tighter at 101 bps. (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0858 GMT [Dow Jones] Switzerland's consumer price index is expected to slip 0.6% year-on-year in December, compared with a 0.5% decline in November, according the average of estimates from five economists compiled by \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\uc2 . Month on month, the index is expected to be unchanged, compared with a 0.2% drop in November. The data are due for release Jan. 6 at 0815 GMT. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0856 GMT [Dow Jones] Italian, Spanish bond yields are steady as the market remains quiet on the last trading day of the year, with volumes extremely light given the holiday period. The two-year Italian bond yield is down 11 basis points at 4.84%, while the 10-year bond yield is close to unchanged at 6.98%, according to Tradeweb. The quiet tone persists elsewhere as well, with the Spanish two-year yield flat at 3.21%, and 10-year down 1 bp at 5.12%. (art.patnaude@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0850 GMT [Dow Jones] Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which stepped in to defend the Polish zloty several times in 2011, can continue doing so in 2012 as its goal is to trim inflation rather than Poland's external debt, BRE says. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has mostly problems with inflation, which with weakening zloty and growth will only tie the monetary council's hands further," BRE says. Dealers mostly tied \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the zloty with a need to trim value the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 -denominated debt ahead of today's fixing in order to prop up debt ratios. EUR/PLN trades at 4.4026. (patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0847 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilt yields keep falling, with another record low for the 10-year note, which is now yielding 1.93%. Banks are deploying cash in gilts on the last trading session of the year although volumes remain very thin. Data already out show UK house prices in December were on average 0.2% lower than the previous month, and 1% higher than the same period last year. March gilts are up 26 ticks at 117.32. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0846 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/TWD is unchanged at 30.290 amid a tug of war between year-end export and import settlements, say two local traders. The pair has lost 0.26% in 2011. The traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was suspected of buying near the close of the onshore trading session, which lifted the pair above 30.270. One of the traders says the USD/TWD is still likely to rise in near term on risk aversion amid lingering worries over Europe's debt problems and tracking the USD/KRW's recent strength; "if local stocks fall next week, the greenback is likely to rise above NT$30.330," he adds. Another trader says, however, the pair is unlikely to move much as foreign investors may not want to make aggressive bets ahead of the local presidential election and Lunar New Year holidays. They tip the pair in a 30.250-30.400 band next week. Taiwan shares end flat, with foreign investors net buying NT$4.98 billion worth of shares. (fanny.liu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0844 GMT [Dow Jones] Europe bank debt insurance costs fall, with volumes remaining low on the final trading day of the year. The iTraxx Europe Senior Financials index is 4 bps tighter at 273/278 bps, according to Markit. It has pushed out around 79 bps since the start of what has been a tumultuous year in the European banking sector. The Sub Financials index is 7 bps tighter at 501/514 bps. It has widened around 152 bps since the beginning of January. (ben.edwards@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds lower in light volumes on the last trading session of the year. The EuroCOIN indicator of euro area economic activity is out at 0830 GMT, while over in the US the ISM-NY business index is due at 1330 GMT. Fewer than 10,000 March futures contracts have been traded so far, and trading is expected to be light for rest of the session as well. March bunds are down 17 ticks at 138.75. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0814 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds are mixed with medium-to-long-term papers in focus, though trading remains thin ahead of the year-end holiday. "There are some last minute trades, most probably to rebalance portfolios, but overall volume is still very low," says a local trader. Investors, especially foreign funds, will likely focus on short-term bonds such as the three-year and five-year bonds in 2012 given easing inflation and the poor economic outlook, he says; "expect some selling pressure in the mid-term papers because there'll be plenty of auctions for the five-to-seven years next year," the trader adds. The yields on September 2016 and July 2021 benchmark papers are both down 1 bp at 3.20% and 3.68% respectively, while the September 2018 MGS yield is flat 3.53%. (jason.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/MYR is unchanged from late Thursday at 3.1760 amid holiday-thinned volumes. Traders say Europe's debt problems and slow economic growth worldwide may continue to weigh on emerging market assets. The pair is tipped to stay in a 3.1500-3.1800 range in the near term. However, with much of the bad news priced in this year, the pair will likely retreat to 3.0500-3.0600 by the end of 2012, says a local trader. "Although our economic fundamental remains good, investors will continue to take a cautious approach due to Europe's debt problems," says the trader. In 1Q 2012, the pair will likely track EUR/USD moves closely, he adds. (jason.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY swaps continue to rise due to the year-end liquidity crunch; one-year \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps are at a premium of 405 pips to the spot exchange rate from 390 pips late Thursday. They're tipped to face resistance at 500 for the session, says a Shanghai-based Asian bank trader; "liquidity remains tight--the market had expected a reserve ratio cut last weekend, but that didn't materialize." The USD/CNY is at 6.2991 from 6.3192 Thursday. Offshore, one-year USD/CNY NDFs are down at 6.3835/6.3855 from 6.3960/6.4000 late Thursday. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0747 GMT [Dow Jones] The USD/CNH, or offshore USD/CNY, edges up amid strong corporate demand for the USD at the year-end, diverging from the lower onshore USD/CNY. The offshore pair is at 6.3480, vs 6.3440 late Thursday. However, the USD/CNH is likely to fall slightly, tracking its onshore counterpart, to touch 6.3430 later in the session, says a trader at a Taiwan bank. The PBOC fixed the central parity sharply (148 bps) lower at 6.3009 from 6.3157 Thursday. Hong Kong Treasury Markets Association's USD/CNH fixing was at 6.3461 vs 6.3390 Thursday. One-year USD/CNY NDFs are at 6.3820/6.3900 vs 6.3960/6.4000 late Thursday. Onshore, the USD/CNY is sharply lower at 6.3010, vs 6.3192 late Thursday. (carol.chan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 30, 2011 04:05 ET (09:05 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020111230e7cu0017n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
