\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Poland \highlight\uc2  keeps rates on hold, warns of more  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2011\par 13:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaves key rate flat, as expected, at 4.5 pct\par \par \uc2 * Bank sees CPI back in target in mid-term, elevated for now\par \par \uc2 * C.bank to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again if zloty "unstable"\par \par \uc2 By Marcin Goettig and Maciej Onoszko\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Dec 7 (Reuters) - Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates on hold again on Wednesday and said it sees inflation returning to target in the medium term, but it warned of more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the zloty continues to be volatile.\par \par \uc2 Rates have now been on hold since June as a weak zloty, which has fallen 12 percent against the euro since June and fuelled inflation, has prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  from cutting interest rates to shore up a slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 In the first half of this year the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its key interest rate by a total of 100 basis points to 4.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The significant monetary policy tightening implemented in the first half of 2011 should enable inflation to return to the target in the medium term," the bank said in a statement after the monthly meeting of the rate-setting Monetary Policy Council.\par \par \uc2 "The impact of the situation in the global financial markets on the zloty exchange rate continues to be an upside risk to domestic price developments," it said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FX INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  four times this year to defend the zloty, most recently in November. The zlotyposting a similar performance to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Hungary that saw its credit rating downgraded to "junk".\par \par \uc2 "We plan to continue our \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  (...) This means we are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  when we believe that there is some destabilisation in the market," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Marek Belka told a conference after the bank's decision.\par \par \uc2 The zloty has been hit by weaker demand for emerging market assets in recent months as the deepening euro zone debt crisis has hurt risk appetite. It has performed as badly as the Hungarian forint, which has been hit by a downgrade of Hungary's credit rating to junk status and concern about Budapest's unorthodox policies.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the zloty gained around 0.2 percent to the euro after Belka's comments and traded at 4.4710 by 1631 GMT.\par \par \uc2 "We do not intend to have any exchange rate target," Belka said.\par \par \uc2 "We are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  so that the risk of hitting a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also present in market participants' balance of risks."\par \par \uc2 He said the bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  curbed volatility in the zloty, which is now weaker than the Czech crown, whereas it was stronger that the crown before the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market for the first time in September.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  will depend on developments in the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 "There are no grounds now to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, neither the volatility nor the exchange rate level justify this," said Mateusz Szczurek, chief CEE economist at ING.\par \par \uc2 "The economic data is still too good to make the MPC cut rates. The probability of a zloty weakening is also too high, so it is hard to consider rate cuts, as long as inflation does not return to levels around 3.5 percent," Szczurek added.\par \par \uc2 Inflation stood at 4.3 percent in October.\par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll on Monday forecast Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep interest rates on hold in the first quarter of 2012 and will then cut rates by a total of 50 basis points next year to support the slowing economy.\par \par \uc2 Poland's economy grew by 4.2 percent year-on-year in the third quarter of 2011, statistics office data showed on Wednesday, just down from 4.3 percent in the second quarter but above a forecast of 4.0 percent.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters expect growth to slow to 3.0 percent in 2012.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Filip Kochan; Writing by Marcin Goettig and Dagmara Leszkowicz; Editing by Susan Fenton)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-RATES/ (UPDATE 2)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111210e7c7000c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
