\par\par \b \uc2 CEE MORNING BRIEFING: Markets Cautious On Risk Appetite Spike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sean Carney   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 894 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Dezember 2011\par 04:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    TODAY'S CALENDAR \par  \par    Local/GMT \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1030/0930 Hungary T-bond auctions\f28 \par \par \uc2 1400/1300 Czech end-November budget balance\par \par \uc2 1500/1400 Czech finance minister at conference\par \par \uc2 N/A Czech, Polish, Hungarian Nov manufacturing PMI data\par \par \uc2 Central European markets will watch the euro zone and United States Thursday, gauging whether regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds will be able to build on gains made Wednesday after six major central banks took coordinated action to boost liquidity.\par \par \uc2 The Czech koruna firmed 1.5% to the euro on the news as risk appetite grew, which in turn pushed Prague stocks 4.2% higher on the day.\par \par \uc2 The volatility shows that it hardly needs much to push the koruna to higher levels, said Michal Brozka, economist at Raiffeisenbank, adding that it also indicates how much the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fundamentally undervalued.\par \par \uc2 Other assets also rose, namely prices on government bonds throughout the region as yields retreated sharply across the board after spiking in some cases as much as 100 basis points in November.\par \par \uc2 Yet developments today are a tough call.\par \par \uc2 "Hopes for a strong rally are rather weak and the positive effect on risky assets is mild," as can be seen from the less dramatic gains made by the zloty and forint on Wednesday, Brozka said.\par \par \uc2 Quoted bond yields sharply retreated from recent highs, but overall trade volumes remain tame as most hot money is instead moving into commodities rather than bonds, said Filip Lesch, a fixed income-trader at CSOB in Prague.\par \par \uc2 In Budapest, Hungary's treasury auctions of bonds maturing in 2014, 2016 and 2020 follow the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's raising its base rate 50 basis points Tuesday to 6.50%, in line with market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The rate hike, however, was below what some on the markets had already priced into Hungarian assets, leading to some disappointment and casting a shadow over Thursday's auction.\par \par \uc2 Separately, Czech finance minister Miroslav Kalousek is slated to participate in a public discussion on tax policy in Prague.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par  \par         Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par         0350 GMT   2150 GMT          High    Low      12/31 \par EUR/CZK 25.162-490 25.250-326 +0.15  25.326   25.313  +1.09 \par EUR/HUF 302.49-5.78 303.07-4.31 +0.15 304.04  303.96  +9.29 \par EUR/PLN 4.4902-5188 4.4999-5090 +0.00 4.5036  4.4968 +13.84 \par  \par FIXED INCOME \par  \par        Hungary        Poland           Czech Republic \par        Wed    Tues    Wed    Tues      Wed    Tues \par 3 yrs  8.65%  9.18%   4.86%  4.97%(1)  2.42%  2.53%(2) \par 5 yrs  8.70%  9.30%   5.19%  5.35%     2.84%  2.94% \par 10yrs  8.70%  9.30%   5.93%  6.04%     4.30%  4.48% \par (1) 2Yrs (2) 4Yrs \par  \par  \par STOCKS \par EU Enlarged 15         1,762.71 gain    93.09    up 5.58% \par WIG20                 2,288.07  gain    105.54   up 4.84% \par BUX                   17,634.70 gain    697.15   up 4.12% \par PX                    878.9    gain     35.5     up  4.2% \par  \par  \par  \par OTHER NEWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Poland's finance ministry will hold a T-bill buy-back auction on Dec. 19 with settlement on Dec. 21, the ministry said in a statement Wednesday. The T-bills to be bought back will be securities maturing in 2012 with a face value of PLN16.75 billion ($5.01 billion), the ministry said.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 POLAND\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : Euro-zone governments hope the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  forcefully in financial markets if the governments agree to ambitious economic governance reforms at their summit next month, Polish Finance Minister Jan Rostowski said Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND: Deleveraging is not the right way for banks to raise capital to achieve their capital ratios, said Jacek Rostowski, finance minister of Poland, which holds the EU rotating presidency. "We reached a very important conclusion that the only way by which banks can build up their capital at the end of June 2012 is by sale of organized parts of their business, but not by winding down business," Rostowski said after the conclusion of a meeting of EU finance ministers.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVAKIA: The Slovak government on Wednesday approved an action plan ahead of a Schengen assessment, which will start in February 2012. According to the plan, there's a need to complete the system of technical and physical protection of Slovakia's external border with Ukraine.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SLOVAKIA: Slovakia will become the first EU country to join the U.S. initiative to combat the smuggling of nuclear materials, as the country passed a joint action plan to curb the illegal use of nuclear and radioactive materials on Wednesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 HUNGARY: Hungary's economy minister plans to consult with the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  governor about its prospects for economic growth next year, a government spokesman said Wednesday. The move is significant as relations between the government and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have long been strained, in part because they have different approaches to economic policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Sean Carney, Dow Jones Newswires; +420 222 315 290; sean.carney@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Gergo Racz in Budapest contributed to this briefing.)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Go to \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/new-europe}}}\uc2 \f2  for the new Dow Jones blog on Central and Eastern Europe, covering business, politics, society and more, written by our correspondents across the region. [ 01-12-11 0358GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1806 75091   \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020111201e7c100007\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
