\par\par \b \uc2 Polish bonds strengthen on talk of finmin buying\b0\par\par\uc2 225 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2011\par 16:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Nov 25 (Reuters) - Polish bond yields fell as much as 13 basis points in late Friday trade on talk that the finance ministry was buying Polish debt on the secondary market, dealers said.\par \par \uc2 Polish debt took a hit earlier in the day after \uc2 Moody's\uc2  cut Hungary's credit rating to "junk", casting a shadow over central and eastern European markets.\par \par \uc2 "There's a rumour that the finance ministry is buying bonds," one Warsaw dealer said.\par \par \uc2 "We're back at the levels at which the market opened."\par \par \uc2 A finance ministry spokeswoman declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 The ministry \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the bond market in late September, boosting prices for the country's debt.\par \par \uc2 There was also talk in the market that the finance ministry had asked various banks about a potential \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the secondary market.\par \par \uc2 "The Finance Ministry asked us today how to positively impact the market. The state-owned bank (that is used by the ministry to conduct purchases of bonds) did not buy bonds today," said one Warsaw-based dealer.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the zloty for the fourth time in less than three months as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  neared multi-month lows.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Dagmara Leszkowicz, Filip Kochan and Marcin Goettig; Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-BONDS/   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111128e7bp0010a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
