\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Polish  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  warns of further  \highlight22\uc2 interventions \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 289 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2011\par 14:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Fewer funds used in last move\par \par \uc2 * More \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely\par \par \uc2 (Adds quote, trader, background)\par \par \uc2 By Pawel Sobczak\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Nov 24 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the weak zloty, and on a larger scale than during its latest move on Wednesday, its deputy governor said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market for the fourth time in less than three months on Wednesday as the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  neared the psychologically key 4.50 line versus the euro, but the move failed to have a sustained effect.\par \par \uc2 "The funds that were used in the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were much smaller than in September and October," Witold Kozinski told Reuters. "The goal of the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not to bring the zloty to a specific level, but to stabilise the market."\par \par \uc2 Kozinski, who is responsible for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Wednesday move was "cautionary".\par \par \uc2 "Further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are likely. This will depend on our view of the market. New \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be conducted on a large scale," he added.\par \par \uc2 The zloty picked up after the comments, dealers said. It was up 0.1 percent against the euro as of 1500 GMT.\par \par \uc2 "The comments by the deputy governor temporarily supported the zloty," one Warsaw trader said. "I think a further retreat of the zloty towards this year's minimum of 4.42-4.53 to the euro is still possible."\par \par \uc2 "The question is whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will defend these levels," he added.\par \par \uc2 The zloty has lost about 11 percent of its value to the euro since June.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Filip Kochan; writing by Chris Borowski)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-ZLOTY/  (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111127e7bo000cu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
