\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging Europe Central Banks Vulnerable To Mounting Attacks\b0\par\par\uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2011\par 22:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall victim to euro-zone debt crisis concerns\par \par \uc2 --Central banks in this region seen with less firepower than other emerging markets to fight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, may continue as these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  underperform\par \par \uc2 By Erin McCarthy\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Having spent much of the past two years trying to weaken their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , emerging-market central banks are now trying to do the opposite, for which they are increasingly tapping the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves they accumulated through those previous actions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some of them, especially those in Eastern and Central Europe, may find that the war chests they built up aren't deep enough to fight off an intensifying attack by speculators as fears of another global crisis grow.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and Romania's central banks have been seen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to prop up their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently, while Turkey's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  holds daily \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -selling auctions to help its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Despite these efforts, the attacks on their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keep growing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The selloff is global, with nervous investors shifting en masse into dollars and safe havens such as the yen. But the problems that poses aren't felt universally.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Latin American countries such as Brazil and Chile and Asian nations such as South Korea and Taiwan all went on aggressive dollar-buying campaigns when the dollar was in constant decline against their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . That has left them in better shape now to address the problem of an ascending dollar, less vulnerable to speculators' attacks, and thus less of a natural target. Even Mexico, which has largely kept out of markets, has about $76 billion of reserves combined with a $72 billion flexible credit line arrangement with the International Monetary Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But emerging European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are under much more pressure, mostly because of their ongoing exposure to the euro zone debt crisis, which crimped their capacity to amass the reserves needed to arrest the mounting attacks on their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , these countries' central banks will have to pit themselves against global investors who likely won't find their reserve levels or other policy tools all that impressive. This will especially be a problem for economies whose reserve levels are marred by large economic imbalances, like Turkey's massive current account deficit and Hungary's high levels of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There aren't too many economies out there that we should be able to expect good things from a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Michael Lee, senior portfolio manager of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  Advantage International Bond Fund.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary holds about $53 billion in reserves, tiny compared to Brazil's $352.9 billion. That weakness, combined with high levels of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  debt--which means a weak forint puts households and banks holding such debt under pressure--has seen investors running for the door from the forint.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With such meager reserves, the Hungarian forint's only defense may be a substantial one-time rate increase, which could hamper already bleak growth. And if it cannot get financial assistance from the International Monetary Fund, as it requested earlier this month, the forint could head for another freefall.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Turkey is in a similar boat. After holding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  selling auctions and directly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market in October, its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ultimately tightened monetary policy with a back-door rate hike, whose effect has since seemingly waned. With about $95 billion in reserves, Turkey must still tread very cautiously, since reserve depletion can unnerve foreign creditors, whose financing helps fund its massive current account deficit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And even Poland, which has better economic fundamentals and sturdier reserve levels at about $110 billion, is struggling to fight the market. Its bank stepped into the market Wednesday to fight off speculative flows hurting the zloty, bringing the euro down as low as PLN4.4423. But the intense selloff across risk assets ultimately erased the effect of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  within hours, leading some to bill it as a failure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, some market watchers say emerging Europe's central banks could catch a break, since dangerously illiquid markets and high trading costs may keep speculators out of the market altogether. Instead, investors may chase \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  whose central banks are standing back. These include the South Africa rand, which has dropped nearly 22% against the dollar since September.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Erin McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2712; erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com [ 23-11-11 2117GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111123e7bn0016m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
