\par\par \b \uc2 Poland - Factors to Watch on Nov 17\b0\par\par\uc2 267 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2011\par 08:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 Here are news stories, press reports and events to watch, which may affect Poland's financial markets on Thursday. ALL TIMES GMT (Poland: GMT + 1 hour):\par \par \uc2 DEFICIT\par \par \uc2 Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Marek Belka expects Prime Minister Tusk to lay out reforms on Friday aimed at curbing the budget deficit to 3 percent of the gross domestic product in 2012, he told Polish public television.\par \par \uc2 MINUTES\par \par \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release the minutes from the November meeting. The bank left interest rates unchanged in November. (1300)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTIONS\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have spent around one billion euros on its two \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September to boost the zloty, according to calculations by Parkiet based on the bank's newly released data.\par \par \uc2 ZLOTY\par \par \uc2 Poland's state-owned BGK bank sold dollars on the spot market in exchange for zlotys in the second such move on Wednesday, three Warsaw-based \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said.\par \par \uc2 WORLD BANK\par \par \uc2 Economic growth in central and eastern Europe will slow to 2.1 percent next year from 3.0 percent in 2011 as the region continues to catch up with the richer members of the European Union, the World Bank said.\par \par \uc2 NOTE - For a diary of forthcoming events see and a calendar of east European economic indicators see .\par \par \uc2 For other related news, double click on: \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Polish equities           E.Eu