\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ/Emerging Europe: Polish Prime Minister To Address Markets In Parliament Speech\b0\par\par\uc2 877 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2011\par 11:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (This article has been posted on Emerging Europe Real Time, The Wall Street Journal Online's site for news and analysis on Central and Eastern Europe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted by Marcin Sobczyk \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Faced with the euro-zone crisis and nudged by his \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Poland's prime minister will talk to the financial markets rather than his voters and lawmakers when he asks parliament on Friday for a vote of confidence. He has little choice but to promise an ambitious fiscal overhaul program designed to slow the growth of public debt, lead to a higher credit rating for Poland, and help it graduate from the emerging markets camp.\par \par \uc2 Poland went bankrupt in the 1980s after several decades of severe mismanagement by communists. Poles may not remember that, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, Marek Belka, told The Wall Street Journal in an interview published this week. But the financial market has a long memory.\par \par \uc2 Since a shock transition to a free market that began in 1989 with the collapse of communism, Poland has been growing rapidly, but has so far failed to restore full trust of the markets. Despite a sound economy and remarkable resilience to crises, the zloty has been strongly volatile since the global crisis that began in 2008. Economists tend to focus more on risks the country faces and less on its proven strengths.\par \par \uc2 Belka has repeatedly urged the cabinet of Donald Tusk to take more action to reduce public deficit. He said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal that a rating upgrade from A-/A2 is within reach if the government presents a credible plan. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor holds a position independent from the government. Since Belka's appointment by the ruling camp in 2010, cabinet officials and the prime minister have lauded cooperation with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Through a combination of higher taxes, lower spending and a cut of cash transfers to private pension funds, the government will likely manage to trim the country's deficit from 7.8% of economic output at the end of last year to likely below 5.6% at the end of December.\par \par \uc2 But that level is high, above the 3% all European Union member states agreed not to exceed. The government has said it's determined to bring the gap below that level by the end of 2012, a goal that will likely be reiterated on Friday. It will be an ambitious one, and exactly what the markets want to hear.\par \par \uc2 According to local press reports here, the prime minister, at the onset of his new term, will promise a set of measures cutting public spending, reducing tax breaks and overhauling the pension system. The government will eradicate childbirth benefits and tax breaks for families with children, daily Rzeczpospolita said this week without citing sources.\par \par \uc2 Social security contributions paid by the self-employed may go up and people in creative professions--like artists and journalists--may have to start paying taxes on all of their income instead of on 50%. The government has said earlier that it would raise value-added taxes further if deficits remain stubbornly high.\par \par \uc2 Polish officials have earlier repeatedly said they would like to be part of the EU core, and in their view this would require a signal that Poland remains committed to joining the euro zone, albeit when the crisis there ends--a distant prospect. According to daily Gazeta Wyborcza, Mr. Tusk wants his country to join the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  grid that keeps \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  floating within a limited range.\par \par \uc2 All countries seeking to adopt the euro are expected to participate in this grid, the European Exchange Rate Mechanism, for at least two years. In the ERMII system, \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are allowed to float within a range of plus or minus 15% to the euro.\par \par \uc2 Participation doesn't automatically lead to euro-zone membership, however. Denmark has been part of the ERMII since 1999, despite having decided to stick with the krone. The Lithuanian litas has been in the mechanism since 2004 and the Latvian lats since 2005.\par \par \uc2 Poland agreed to join the euro zone before it entered the European Union in May 2004. But the euro-zone debt crisis has complicated the plan, possibly delaying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  switch until 2019, the country's finance minister said earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Tusk's speech on Friday is unlikely to signal any significant steps toward abandoning the zloty. But Poland's entry into the ERMII exchange rate mechanism could stabilize its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that now requires regular government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit volatility. It could also give \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 --the largest economy in the EU's eastern flank and the second-largest EU member outside the single-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  union--a stronger voice on EU and euro-zone matters.\par \par \uc2 During Tusk's first term in power, Poland's per-capita wealth versus the EU average has likely jumped 10 percentage points to about 64%. The prime minister now needs to make sure the country grows at least as fast in his second term, in an increasingly unstable external environment.\par \par \uc2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 . [ 17-11-11 1033GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111117e7bh000qj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
