\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Polish zloty within acceptable range-deputy cbank head\b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2011\par 07:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2 * Zloty should be stronger\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could finance domestic purchases of Polish lenders\par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Nov 16 (Reuters) - The Polish zloty\par \par \uc2 is trading within an acceptable rang but should be stronger based on its fundamentals, the deputy head of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted as saying on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The unit has lost more than 10 percent of its value since the start of June as the euro zone debt crisis fueled investors flight from riskier assets.\par \par \uc2 "When it comes to fundamentals, it (the zloty) should be stronger," Witold Kozinski said in an interview published by Warsaw daily Rzeczpospolita. "But considering what is happening currently on the markets, I expect the zloty to remain around the current level."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  three times this year -- twice in September and once in October -- to prop up the zloty and, according to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's governor, it would not hesitate to step into the spot market further to limit speculation.\par \par \uc2 Polish authorities are anxious to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from a sharp weakening at the end of the year as almost one-third of Poland's debt is in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a sharp fall of the unit would mean breaching the key debt level of 55 percent of GDP.\par \par \uc2 Kozinski also said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help finance the purchase of Polish units put up for sale by troubled foreign parents in order to increase local participation in the financial sector.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Marek Belka and other Polish officials have said they hope the sales would allow domestic players to reduce foreign ownership of the local banking sector from the current 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Chris Borowski and Dagmara Leszkowicz; Editing by Richard Borsuk)\par \par \uc2 POLAND-ZLOTY (UPDATE 1)   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020111119e7bg0004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
