\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ: Polish Bank Chief Builds Defenses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gordon Fairclough And\u8201\'20\'09 Marcin Sobczyk   \par \uc2 Of THE WALL STREET JOURNAL   \par \uc2 2,408 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2011\par 06:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2011 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--Poland should strengthen its defenses against financial contagion from the euro zone by tightening public spending and making plans to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Western European banks with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  here start to falter, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief said.\par \par \uc2 "We're bracing ourselves for a slowdown," said Marek Belka, governor of the National Bank of Poland. Still, he said, he is confident the country "will be crisis-proof in its ability to avoid a recession," even if conditions in the neighboring euro area deteriorate further.\par \par \uc2 So far, the Polish economy has proved remarkably resilient. Poland was the only European Union nation that didn't slip into recession after the start of the 2008 global financial crisis. This year, gross domestic product is on track to expand around 4%, but the pace of growth is likely to slow in 2012.\par \par \uc2 Even so, Mr. Belka called on the government to push ahead with a "more transparent and ambitious fiscal policy" to shore up state finances and win investor approval.\par \par \uc2 "A rating upgrade is clearly within reach, so why not grab it," Mr. Belka said in an interview. "There's a chance for Poland to be clearly on the right side of the bond market" and to avoid the mistakes of Italy, whose borrowing costs have soared recently because of investors' doubts about the country's commitment to austerity.\par \par \uc2 "Two months earlier, even two weeks earlier, a clear program of reforms would have changed the game" in Italy, Mr. Belka said. These days, Mr. Belka said, "you have to be ahead of the curve, pre-empt the markets."\par \par \uc2 Poland is in far stronger financial shape than Italy, whose public debt is equivalent to about 120% of annual economic output. At the end of 2010, Poland's public debt stood at about 55% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 This year, the general government deficit is set to shrink to 5.6%. The government says it is aiming for a budget gap of less than 3% next year, but the European Commission has said Poland is unlikely to hit that target without deeper cuts in spending, revenue increases or both.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Belka, who has previously served as finance minister and prime minister of Poland, said pushing through wide-ranging structural change is never easy. But he said now there "is an ideal environment for sending a clear signal to the markets."\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Donald Tusk, whose coalition government was re-elected with a solid parliamentary majority last month, has political capital and a respite from elections. He has pledged strong measures to close the budget deficit.\par \par \uc2 One way the euro crisis could affect Poland, is through its banks, Mr. Belka said. Roughly 70% of Poland's banking assets are controlled by Western European banks. "We're looking at this with some concern," Mr. Belka said. He said that overall, banks in Poland are well-capitalized, liquid and relatively free of bad loans. But he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was worried about "those banks that may become orphans because their parent banks are in trouble."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Belka said Polish government institutions and local investors should be prepared to step in if that happens. "We should use the resilience of Polish capital to inject funds," he said, adding that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stand ready to provide liquidity if necessary.\par \par \uc2 "I'm not looking for the state to become a shareholder-that's absolutely a last-resort option," Mr. Belka said. But he said Poland has private capital that could be used to put a larger share of the country's banking sector into Polish hands. "There's nothing unusual for a country Poland's size to think about such an evolution," he said.\par \par \uc2 Belgium's \uc2 KBC Group NV\uc2  and Portugal's Banco Comercial Portugues have said they are considering sales of their Polish holdings. Others are also considering selling Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Polish bankers say. Poland's largest lender, \uc2 PKO Bank Polski\uc2  SA, and insurer \uc2 PZU SA\uc2 , are potential buyers.\par \par \uc2 On interest rates, Mr. Belka said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which last Wednesday held the benchmark rate steady at 4.5%, has leeway to raise or cut as conditions warrant.\par \par \uc2 "We'd like to keep the economy afloat, with a decent growth rate, but at the same time keep inflation firmly in grip," he said. "If we manage it, we will emerge from the crisis much stronger."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Belka said the bank resisted rate-cut calls earlier this year from observers worried about the impact of the euro-zone slowdown. After the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut rates, Mr. Belka said: "We sent a signal. Just hold on a minute. We are fighting inflation. Don't forget inflation. We want to keep growing in a healthy way."\par \par \uc2 -By Gordon Fairclough And\u8201\'20\'09 Marcin Sobczyk, The Wall Street Journal; Gordon.Fairclough@wsj.com, Marcin.Sobczyk@wsj.com [ 14-11-11 0501GMT ]\par \par \uc2 (Emerging Europe Real Time, The Wall Street Journal Online's site for news and analysis on Central and Eastern Europe, can be found at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.wsj.com/emergingeurope}}}\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Posted by Marcin Sobczyk \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WARSAW--The National Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent the zloty from weakening rapidly have produced an encouraging result and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is ready to step in again if investors exit the Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a disorderly fashion, Marek Belka, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governor, said in an interview with The Wall Street Journal.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Here is the transcript of his comments:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZLOTY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTIONS\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've shown that we wouldn't shy away from emerging in the market and the markets know that they should in their behavior take into account the risk of an NBP \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . At the same time, we've also stressed that we're not trying to defend any specific exchange rate level.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "What we're determined to fight is disorderly behavior. If we see something not substantiated by global tendencies--something that is Poland-specific and unfounded, and not based on our fundamentals--then we'll step in. We've chosen a moment like this a few weeks ago and the result is encouraging for the moment."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A catastrophic event in the euro zone, which Poland doesn't wish for, would likely weaken the zloty at first, but Poland's sound economy and proven resistance to crises could eventually prompt investors to buy the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The immediate effect would probably be the usual one, which is the weakening of the zloty as the markets react in an instinctive way, escaping to safe havens and most liquid markets, which is the U.S. market. I wouldn't, however, assume away a reversal and an opposite movement, strengthening the zloty, because the Polish economy seems to be relatively crisis-resistant and then it will be reflected in the zloty.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But of course we'd rather not take the risk of this tsunami. Surfing a tsunami is always a very risky business."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLISH ECONOMIC PROSPECTS:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "My perception of the prospects of the economy isn't bleak at all. Even with a very bad scenario in the euro zone, the Polish economy will be crisis-proof in its ability to avoid a recession. Of course, we're bracing ourselves for a slowdown, but I think it won't be as bad as the pessimists expect.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If you stay in Brussels too long, you come back with gloom. There's more sunshine in Poland now than in other places. It doesn't mean we are entirely unaffected by what is happening."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND'S CREDIT RATINGS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We deserve a higher credit rating. For that, we need a more transparent and ambitious fiscal policy. This is something we all expect from the new government. It's not just about playing with the numbers, but also about structural reforms. Poland isn't the most unreformed country in the European Economic Area. We have many strengths, but we also have weaknesses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Prime Minister has recently confirmed in private what he says in public: that he's determined to present a plan of far-reaching reforms, which may be difficult. It's very difficult to propose far-reaching reforms in Poland because we're saying we're not in a crisis---and we're not, but people will then ask what the reform is for.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But here you have to be ahead of the curve, pre-empt the markets. You have to be on the right side of the bond markets. What we see now in Italy is the exact opposite to what should have been done. Two months earlier, even two weeks earlier, a clear program of reforms would change the game. Now, it will cost them much more, and us too, of course.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I hope the government understands this. We hope for an ambitious reform plan. You don't have to do everything all at once. But this is optimal timing. The government has three years with no elections, they have a clear parliamentary majority, the opposition in disarray, and the economy is doing much better than all expected. This is an ideal environment for sending a clear signal to the markets that this is a country fit for an upgrade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's a chance for Poland to be clearly on the right side of the bond market. I don't think we'll waste this opportunity. It won't be easy but the alternative is very costly. A rating upgrade is clearly within reach, so why not grab it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The zloty could revert to where it belongs---clearly it is undervalued now, which helps exports. But if the zloty weakened, then it should come back by something close to that."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND'S MONETARY POLICY\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "As far as monetary policy is concerned, Poland is in a pretty comfortable position. With our interest rates, we can go both ways, depending of what's necessary. We haven't put ourselves in the corner of ultra-low rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're one of the few countries in the advanced world that is serious about fighting inflation. We're betting for the whole stake. We'd like to keep the economy afloat, with a decent growth rate, but at the same time keep inflation firmly in grip. If we manage it, we'll emerge from the crisis much stronger. We'll keep growing, in a healthy way. When inflation gets out of control, the cost of reducing it to sustainable levels is immense.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A few weeks ago, the markets were pleading for a rate reduction, because of the tsunami from Western Europe. The ECB cut rates. But we sent a signal. Just hold on a minute. We are fighting inflation. Don't forget inflation. We want to keep growing in a healthy way. Low inflation is one thing, moderate dependence on foreign capital is another. It's a vulnerable spot in the Polish economy. If you compare averages, we look very good. But the fact is that most of the increase in public debt last year and this year was financed with foreign capital. This is obviously vulnerability and we have to do something about it."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 POLAND SHOULD PREPARE FOR BANKING SECTOR ORPHANS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There are many channels through which the crisis may propagate itself. I'm less worried about the trade channel. The real unknown is how the European banking sector will survive this. We cannot forget that most of our banks in Poland are subsidiaries of European banking groups. We're looking at this with some concern.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our banks very well capitalized and free of toxic assets. Non-performing loans are moderate, at worst. The banks are well provisioned and liquid. However, in these days you don't look at averages. You look at the weakest links in the chain, at those banks that may become orphans because their parent banks are in trouble. And we look at those banks that are excessively engaged in foreign-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  lending.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The flow is very moderate now. But there is the stock, which clearly has the potential to destabilize those banks. Even a minor problem is something you have to worry about these days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Poles have forgotten that 20 years ago we were a bankrupt. We weren't in the situation of Greece---everything was different---but we benefited from debt reduction. Without a very smart licensing policy and opening the banking sector to foreign investors, we would have had a banking crisis.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Those banks have been, by and large, very good corporate citizens of this country. They've behaved in an exemplary way. But some of those banks could be orphaned.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're not talking about nationalizing them, but we should look at domestic capital. We should use the resilience of Polish capital to inject funds. There's nothing unusual for a country Poland's size to think about such an evolution. The country should be prepared for it.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We should look at private equity, there's enough money now of various origins that can be mobilized by private equity funds. Don't forget PZU, which almost publicly admitted that if there is an opportunity, they'll look at it. If the price is right, the insurer wouldn't be loath to participate. Of course there are some banks with Polish identity, but I wouldn't treat this as the first option.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't have a plan in the sense that there is a ready buyer looking to grab low hanging fruit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In this country, we've been educated in the transition to the market. In many respects, we've learned the lessons too eagerly. We have believed all the fairy tales about the free-market economy, so now anything that smells of economic nationalism looks dirty---but just look around. It's like Keynes said: When the facts change, I change my mind.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're not talking about grabbing the whole sector. But if there is a situation where a foreign-owned bank becomes an orphan, we should be ready to take action. What's more important here is support for such solutions. Our bank can play a role if necessary. We can provide necessary liquidity, which is by definition short-term. I'm not looking for the state to become a shareholder - that's absolutely a last resort option."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -For continuously updated news from The Wall Street Journal, see WSJ.com at \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://wsj.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://wsj.com}}}\uc2 \f2 . [ 14-11-11 1014GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020111114e7be00099\par 